canonical-commutation-relation-e0328594.conceptual_definition:
The Canonical Commutation Relation is a fundamental postulate in quantum mechanics that defines the relationship between the position operator 
($
\hat{x}$
) and the momentum operator 
($
\hat{p}$
) in a given direction. It quantifies the inherent uncertainty or non-commutativity in simultaneously measuring these conjugate variables. Specifically, it states that the commutator of 
($
\hat{x}$
) and 
($
\hat{p}$
), which is defined as 
($
\hat{x}
\hat{p} - 
\hat{p}
\hat{x}$
), is equal to the imaginary unit 
($
i$
